formatted_question:
According to the paper "Controlling Text-to-Image Diffusion by Orthogonal Finetuning", how is the hyperspherical energy of a fully connected layer $\bm{W}$ defined in terms of its normalized neurons $\hat{\bm{w}}_i$?
(A) \text{HE}(\bm{W}):= \sum_{i\neq j} \left(1-\hat{\bm{w}}_i^\top \hat{\bm{w}}_j\right)^{-1}
(B) \text{HE}(\bm{W}):= \sum_{i\neq j} \|\hat{\bm{w}}_i-\hat{\bm{w}}_j\|^{-1}
(C) \text{HE}(\bm{W}):= \sum_{i\neq j} \|\bm{w}_i-\bm{w}_j\|^{-1}
(D) \text{HE}(\bm{W}):= \sum_{i\neq j} \|\hat{\bm{w}}_i-\hat{\bm{w}}_j\|
(E) \text{HE}(\bm{W}):= \sum_{i=1}^{n} \|\hat{\bm{w}}_i-\hat{\bm{w}}^0_i\|^{-1}

raw_knowledge_statement:
OFT can be interpreted as minimizing the hyperspherical energy difference between the finetuned model and the pretrained model:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:oft_principle}
\footnotesize
\min_{\bm{W}} \left\| \text{HE}(\bm{W})-\text{HE}(\bm{W}^0)\right\|~~\Leftrightarrow~~\min_{\bm{W}} \bigg{\|} \sum_{i\neq j} \|\hat{\bm{w}}_i-\hat{\bm{w}}_j\|^{-1}-\sum_{i\neq j} \|\hat{\bm{w}}^0_i-\hat{\bm{w}}^0_j\|^{-1}\bigg{\|}
\end{equation}

where $\thickmuskip=2mu \medmuskip=2mu \hat{\bm{w}}_i:=\bm{w}_i/\|\bm{w}_i\|$ denotes the $i$-th normalized neuron, and the hyperspherical energy of a fully connected layer $\bm{W}$ is defined as $\thickmuskip=2mu \medmuskip=2mu \text{HE}(\bm{W}):= \sum_{i\neq j} \|\hat{\bm{w}}_i-\hat{\bm{w}}_j\|^{-1}$.

Formally, OFT seeks to optimize the orthogonal matrix $\thickmuskip=2mu \medmuskip=2mu \bm{R}\in\mathbb{R}^{d \times d}$ for a pretrained fully connected layer $\thickmuskip=2mu \medmuskip=2mu\bm{W}^0\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}$, changing the forward pass from $\thickmuskip=2mu \medmuskip=2mu\bm{z}=(\bm{W}^0)^\top\bm{x}$ to

\begin{equation}\label{eq:oft_general}
    \footnotesize
    \bm{z}=\bm{W}^\top\bm{x}=(\bm{R}\cdot\bm{W}^0)^\top\bm{x},~~~\text{s.t.}~\bm{R}^\top\bm{R}=\bm{R}\bm{R}^\top=\bm{I}
\end{equation}

where $\bm{W}$ denotes the OFT-finetuned weight matrix and $\bm{I}$ is an identity matrix.

formatted_question:
According to the paper "Controlling Text-to-Image Diffusion by Orthogonal Finetuning", in the illustrative example of a fully connected layer with weight matrix $\bm{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}$ and input vector $\bm{x}\in\mathbb{R}^d$, how is the output vector $\bm{z}\in\mathbb{R}^n$ computed?
(A) \bm{z}=\bm{W}\bm{x}
(B) \bm{z}=\bm{W}^\top\bm{x}+\bm{b}
(C) \bm{z}=(\bm{W}^0)^\top\bm{x}
(D) \bm{z}=\bm{W}^\top\bm{x}
(E) \bm{z}=\bm{x}^\top\bm{W}

According to the paper "Controlling Text-to-Image Diffusion by Orthogonal Finetuning", when the minimum of the hyperspherical energy matching objective is achieved up to a rotation or reflection, what equation gives the relationship between the fully connected layer weights $\bm{W}$ and the pretrained weights $\bm{W}^0$?
(A) \bm{W}=\bm{W}^0+\bm{A}\bm{B}^\top
(B) \bm{W}=\bm{W}^0\bm{R}
(C) \bm{W}=(\bm{I}+\bm{R})\bm{W}^0
(D) \bm{W}=\bm{R}\bm{W}^0
(E) \bm{W}=\bm{W}^0-\eta\nabla_{\bm{W}}\mathcal{L}

According to the paper "Controlling Text-to-Image Diffusion by Orthogonal Finetuning", in the illustrative example of a fully connected layer $\bm{W}=\{\bm{w}_1,\cdots,\bm{w}_n\}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}$, what space does each neuron $\bm{w}_i$ belong to?
(A) \mathbb{R}^{1\times d}
(B) \mathbb{R}^{n}
(C) \mathbb{R}^{d\times n}
(D) \mathbb{R}^{n\times d}
(E) \mathbb{R}^{d}

